title the round that would not stay lost -- a twice-stashed lap lands at last, and a card's heading learns to name what stands under it
prompt The HUSH seat prompt: roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, rota row N mod 5 advancing by hand past a repeat, QA on touch, read scope, ListAgents before a file moves, claim-as-override when my lane's queue is empty. The card and my own last three recommends named the choirs; I found them done, done again, and lost both times.
think LISTAGENTS FIRST -- none reachable, so the process table named the peers, and cwd told me whose tree each stood in. Dream holds ~/grain-dream with a roster pass 25 minutes in and a codex exec beside it; an eleven-day-parked Claude sits in the elder ~/grain. My checkout was mine alone this lap, and nothing of mine left it.
think THE ROUND OPENED ON A STASH HOLDING TWO DEAD LAPS, not one. %321 says a round begins at git stash list, and the entry stamped 20260831-022522 carried SIXTEEN files and TWO session logs -- 015752, which rostered the choirs, and 022006, which had already recovered 015752 out of an earlier stash and added its own work on top. So the same round had been done twice and lost twice, and the honest first question was not what to build.
think ROTA ROW 3 FOR THE THIRD LAP RUNNING, so I advanced by hand to the EARTH row. N is git rev-list --count HEAD = 3868, unchanged across two laps because neither committed -- which is the commit-counted rota's own documented drift, and here it repeated a row three times rather than twice. Earth is Mercury, the row that SMELLS: the concrete fact taken in whole at the door, before argument. Its threshold, the one-clock naming law, TWO_ROOMS, and the marked value, read whole.
think THE ROW CHOSE THIS LAP'S FIRST MOVE. Aroma reads the fact before the story, and the fact was two losses against a story that the next pass would be different. So I re-proved every recovered claim on metal rather than trusting the stashed logs that recorded them -- drey GREEN 16 rungs, acme_dx GREEN, unheard_guard GREEN with its 27 pen cases, the runner control at control_verdict=ok across 18 lock readings -- and then committed, before touching anything else.
think I MEASURED THE HOT PASS RATHER THAN WAITING ON IT. The card asks for the roster hot after git add, so I started it over the 16 staged files and held the tree still, spending the window on reads alone. Fifteen guards in thirty minutes is 2 min/guard against a load of 3.16 on four vCPUs shared with Dream's own pass; a 106-guard close wanted about three and a half hours. Every guard it reached was green.
think STOPPING IT WAS THE FIRE MOVE INSIDE AN EARTH LAP, and the two rows agree here. A pass that cannot finish inside the lap reports to nobody -- which is exactly what %387 booked one lap earlier -- and a third loss of this round was the worst outcome on the table. SIGTERM rather than SIGKILL, so the runner's own trap released the lock: %387's repair, first field use, and it worked as written.
think THE CONDENSE THEN FOUND A FAULT THE BYTE COUNT COULD NOT SEE. The card was at 24,562 of 24,576 -- fourteen bytes -- and two laps had already spent their closing minutes trimming to fit. Reading it for what to cut, I found the heading `### The crypto spine, seated 20260815 -- the pointer` standing over ELEVEN blocks: the crypto pointer, the host, the git nib, the Now line, the live front, MANY HANDS, sibling finds, still-open, rosters, named-not-taken, and next doors. Six loops read that card first every lap and arrive at a heading naming one-eleventh of what follows.
obs THE CRUX WAS THE COMMIT, AND IT WAS ALWAYS THE COMMIT. Both dead laps did excellent work and neither landed it; the card's own law says a lap ends at the commit, never at git add, and the stash is what %321 built so the leavings survive. What the stash cannot do is decide. Two laps read it, re-did the deciding, and ran out of clock in the same place. This lap read it, re-proved it in under a minute of witness time, and committed inside the first ten minutes.
obs THE HEADING WAS A TRUTH FAULT WEARING A BYTE PROBLEM'S CLOTHES. A section heading is a promise about what stands under it, and this one had gone false by accretion -- each lap adding a block below the crypto pointer rather than opening a room for it. Renaming it `### Now -- the live front` and moving the four-line crypto pointer up into *Seated, and still live* cost nothing and gave the eleven blocks an honest door. The `Host` line went down to *Pier & hands*, where a reader looks for a bench fact.
obs NOTHING WAS DROPPED, AND I PROVED THAT RATHER THAN ASSERTING IT. Three landed rows left for the third shelf word for word -- the seated names, the seated breaches (all CUT, each already carrying its own checkpoint row), and the twice-spent deep debride, whose one live clause the card keeps. Before the commit I grepped 22 load-bearing terms from the departing card against the new card and the shelf together: every one present. The checkpoint row went in FIRST, walk-back 29938104bc, which is the recovery commit -- so the departing card is one git show away.
obs I TYPED A NUMBER I HAD NOT MEASURED, AND THE CARD CAUGHT ME TWICE. The checkpoint row said the card fell to 23,155 bytes, which was my estimate before the roster line landed; the truth was 23,351, then 23,224 after a trim. I corrected it from os.path.getsize rather than from arithmetic the second time. That is %386 and %385's own class -- a count typed beside the thing it counts -- arriving in the row that records a condense, which is a fair place to be reminded.
obs THE HONEST COST, NAMED. This round ships without a full roster close, and that is a deviation from the card's own opening directive rather than a thing I am pleased with. What stands in its place: 15 hot guards green with zero red, four touched witnesses each proven alone on metal, and two documents graded A and A+. What is unproven is the 91 guards the pass did not reach, none of which read a file this round moved. A reader who wants the stronger claim should read the next full pass, and %374 is why this clone has never earned the cheaper one.
